    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d40">

                <pb facs="0133" n="37" xml:id="pg37"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00 R 29/552: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Wallace</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-03-23T14:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 23,
                                1921—2 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>165. For General Allen,<anchor n="29" xml:id="d40-f1"/> Coblenz.</p>
                <p>In response to your various telegrams<anchor n="30" xml:id="d40-f2"/> on the
                    subject of Allied customs control in the American zone, you are informed that
                    although this Government will take no part in the enforcement of penalties
                    decided on by the Allies, it does not wish to put unnecessary obstacles in the
                    way of such enforcement. This Government, therefore, perceives no objection to
                    the placing of Allied customs collectors at the request of the Rhineland High
                    Commission in the Rhine bridgehead in the American zone if you consider that
                    this will in no way endanger the safety of the American forces of occupation nor
                    interfere with the authority vested in you under armistice conditions.</p>
                <p>Secretary of War has been informed of this telegram.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d40-f1" n="29" xml:id="d40-f1-content">
                <p>Commander of the American Army of Occupation in Germany; American observer on the
                    Rhineland High Commission.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d40-f2" n="30" xml:id="d40-f2-content">
